    <ferc:OrderNumber id="f-1508" contextRef="c-303" unitRef="u-12" decimals="0">7</ferc:OrderNumber>
    <ferc:OrderNumber id="f-1509" contextRef="c-304" unitRef="u-12" decimals="0">8</ferc:OrderNumber>
    <ferc:NotesToFinancialStatementsTextBlock id="f-1511" contextRef="c-14">&lt;div class="Section_1"&gt; 			&lt;div style="clear: both"&gt; 				&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0; font-size: 14pt"&gt; 					&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;New York Transco LLC&lt;/span&gt; 				&lt;/p&gt; 				&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0; font-size: 12pt"&gt; 					&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;Notes to Financial Statements&lt;/span&gt; 				&lt;/p&gt; 				&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 18pt; border-bottom: 0.75pt solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1); padding-bottom: 1pt; font-size: 12pt"&gt; 					&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;December 31, 2025 and 2024&lt;/span&gt; 				&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;/div&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-indent: -28.8pt; page-break-after: avoid; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: &amp;quot;Arial Bold&amp;quot;; font-weight: bold"&gt;1.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 20.46pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;Nature of Operations&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;New York Transco&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;LLC (the &amp;quot;Company&amp;quot; or &amp;quot;NY Transco&amp;quot;), a New York limited liability company (&amp;quot;LLC&amp;quot;) was formed in November&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;2014, by affiliates of the four investor owned electric utilities in New York, to develop and own new electric transmission projects for the New York bulk electric system. The members of the Company and their respective ownership percentages for projects under development and in-service, with the exception of the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 1)"&gt;Propel NY Energy Alternate Solution 5 (the &amp;quot;Propel Project&amp;quot;) as described below, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;are as follows: Consolidated Edison Transmission,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;LLC (&amp;quot;CET&amp;quot;) (45.652%); Grid NY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;LLC (&amp;quot;Grid NY&amp;quot;) (28.261%); Avangrid Networks New York TransCo,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;LLC (&amp;quot;Avangrid&amp;quot;) (19.973%); and Central Hudson Electric Transmission LLC (&amp;quot;CHET&amp;quot;) (6.114%) (collectively, the &amp;quot;Members&amp;quot;). The ownership percentages for the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 1)"&gt;Propel Project are as follows: CET&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt; (41.766%); Grid NY (28.261%); Avangrid (19.973%); and CHET (10.000%). All the Company's transmission assets are under the operational control of the New York Independent System Operator,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;Inc. (the &amp;quot;NYISO&amp;quot;).&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;The Limited Liability Company Agreement (&amp;quot;LLCA&amp;quot;), as amended, governs the formation and business purpose of the Company and identifies the types of electric transmission projects that the Company can propose and develop.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;In December 2015, the New York State Public Service Commission (the &amp;quot;NYSPSC&amp;quot;) issued an order in its competitive proceeding to select transmission projects that would relieve transmission congestion between upstate and downstate and could cost up to $1 billion (the &amp;quot;AC Proceeding&amp;quot;). In its order, the NYSPSC determined that there is a Public Policy Transmission Need (&amp;quot;PPTN&amp;quot;) to address the congestion and directed the NYISO to conduct a solicitation for projects. Consistent with the NYSPSC order, the NYISO solicitation requested proposals for development of two new transmission segments, Segment A and Segment B, to address the PPTN. National Grid submitted a proposal on behalf of the Company (the &amp;quot;NY Transco AC Project Proposal&amp;quot;). The NYISO evaluated the submitted projects under its Federal Energy Regulatory Commission (the &amp;quot;FERC&amp;quot; or &amp;quot;Commission&amp;quot;) approved &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 1)"&gt;Public Policy Transmission Planning Process (the &amp;quot;NYISO PPTPP&amp;quot;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt; and, in October 2016, submitted a list of viable and sufficient projects, one of which was the NY Transco AC Project Proposal, to the NYSPSC for its determination as to whether the transmission need still exists. In January 2017, the NYSPSC found that the PPTN still exists, and directed the NYISO to proceed with selection. On April 8, 2019, the NYISO Board of Directors announced the selection of the Company to develop the Segment B portion of the NY Transco AC Project Proposal (the &amp;quot;Segment B Project&amp;quot;). The cost recovery for this project was initially established by settlement agreement filed in FERC Docket No. ER15-572-000 and approved by FERC on November 16, 2017 (the &amp;quot;Settlement Agreement&amp;quot;). Following its selection by the NYISO and in accordance with the Settlement Agreement, on May 8, 2019, NY Transco submitted revised tariff records to the Transco Facilities Charge under Schedule 13 (Section 6.13) and the Formula Rate and proposed cost allocation methodology in Attachment DD (Section 36) of the NYISO Open Access Transmission Tariff (&amp;quot;OATT&amp;quot; or &amp;quot;Tariff&amp;quot;) to effectuate each of the terms of the Settlement Agreement. The Commission accepted the compliance filing by Delegated Order on October 29, 2019. As contemplated in the Settlement Agreement, the Company purchased the development rights from National Grid for approximately $17.3 million, of which approximately $16.3 million was charged to construction work in progress and the balance to Other expense. During the second quarter of 2019, the Company began the permitting and siting process under New York's Public Service Law Article VII process. Construction began in March 2021 on the first phase of the New York Energy Solution portion (the &amp;quot;NYES Project&amp;quot;) of the Segment B Project, and construction on the remaining phase of the NYES Project started in the second half of 2021 after receipt of the final approval, and the NYES Project was placed into service in June 2023. &lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;On February 23, 2023, the NYISO submitted a First Amended and Restated Development Agreement in FERC Docket No. ER23-1168-000 that, among other things, added the installation of two new 750 MVA 345 kV phase angle regulators at a new station (&amp;quot;Dover PAR Station&amp;quot;) as part of the Segment B Project. FERC accepted the agreement for filing in a delegated order dated April 14, 2023. The Dover PAR Station portion of the Segment B Project received municipal approvals from the Town of Dover Planning Board (the &amp;quot;Dover Approval&amp;quot;) in the first quarter of 2023 and certain construction activities began. Subsequent to the Dover Approval, opposition groups commenced a special court proceeding seeking to annul the Dover Approval. On August 24, 2023, the court found in favor of the opposition groups vacating and nullifying the Dover Approval (the &amp;quot;August 2023 Ruling&amp;quot;) and remanded the matter back to the Town of Dover Planning Board (the &amp;quot;Dover Planning Board&amp;quot;) for review. After working with the Dover Planning Board to address the items identified in the August 2023 Ruling, the Company received approval from the Dover Planning Board and construction activities resumed during the second quarter of 2024, with a goal to be in-s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 1)"&gt;ervice by July 2025. The NYISO submitted a Second Amended and Restated Development Agreement with FERC on January 12, 2024, to reflect the new in-service date for the Dover PAR Station and the Commission accepted the agreement for filing in a delegated order dated March 11, 2024. On July 18, 2025, the NYISO submitted a Third Amended and Restated Development Agreement in FERC Docket No. ER25-2891-000 to reflect a new Required Project In-Service Date for the Dover PAR Station of December 31, 2025.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 1)"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 1)"&gt;The Commission accepted the agreement for filing in a delegated order dated September 15, 2025.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 1)"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 1)"&gt;The Rock Tavern to Sugarloaf portion (the &amp;quot;RTS Project&amp;quot; or the &amp;quot;Segment B Additions&amp;quot;) of the Segment B Project received its final approvals and construction began in June 2022 and was placed into service in June 2023.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span style="background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 1)"&gt;In October 2021, the Company, jointly with the New York Power Authority (&amp;quot;NYPA&amp;quot;), submitted proposed solutions in response to the NYISO's solicitation for Public Policy Transmission Projects to address the Long Island Offshore Wind Export Public Policy Transmission Need (the &amp;quot;LI PPTN Solicitation&amp;quot;) as part of the NYISO's PPTPP.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 1)"&gt;On June 20, 2023, the NYISO Board of Directors announced the selection of the Propel Project, one of the solutions jointly submitted by the Company and NYPA, as the more efficient or cost-effective solution. The Propel Project will improve the transfer capability between Long Island and the rest of NYISO's Control Area and provide additional reliability benefits to Long Island and New York City, with an expected in-service date of 2030. The Company's share of the Propel Project was estimated to be approximately $2.3 billion, excluding interconnection costs and costs associated with transmission upgrade projects expected to be built by local transmission owners. On October 27, 2023, the Company submitted a filing pursuant to sections 205 and 219 of the Federal Power Act (&amp;quot;FPA&amp;quot;) in FERC Docket No. ER24-232-000 requesting (i) approval of a proposed cost allocation methodology associated with its investment in the Propel Project; (ii) approval of a proposed cost containment mechanism for recovery of Included Capital Costs above the cost estimate, plus an escalation factor, that was included as part of the Propel Project solicitation submission; (iii) approval to establish a base return on equity (&amp;quot;ROE&amp;quot;) of 10.7% for the Propel Project to be applied in the Company's Formula Rate in Section 36, Attachment DD of the NYISO OATT; (iv) approval of certain electric transmission rate incentive treatments associated with its investment in the Propel Project (including a) recovery of 100% of prudently incurred costs in the event the Propel Project must be cancelled or abandoned for reasons beyond the Company's control (&amp;quot;Abandoned Plant Incentive&amp;quot;); b) inclusion of 100% of constriction work in progress in rate base during the development and construction phase (&amp;quot;CWIP Incentive&amp;quot;); c) a 150 basis point adder to its base ROE component to compensate for the significant risks and challenges associated with the development of the Propel Project (&amp;quot;Risks and Challenges Adder&amp;quot;); and d) a 50 basis point adder to its base ROE component for the Company's voluntary participation in NYISO (&amp;quot;RTO Participation Adder&amp;quot;)); and (v) acceptance of additional minor revisions to Sections 6.13 and Section 36, Attachment DD of the NYISO OATT to include the Propel Project as an eligible transmission asset for cost recovery purposes (&amp;quot;Application&amp;quot;). On December 26, 2023, the FERC issued an order accepting the Company's filing, and approving the Abandoned Plant Incentive, the CWIP Incentive, and the RTO Participation Adder, and also approving a 75 basis point Risk and Challenges Adder. &lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span style="background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 1)"&gt;The Commission also set the base ROE component for hearing and settlement judge procedures. The Company reached a settlement with the Parties&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 1)"&gt;and filed an uncontested Offer of Settlement with FERC on August 21, 2024 (the &amp;quot;Propel Offer of Settlement&amp;quot;). The Propel Offer of Settlement resolves all issues set for hearing or pending in requests for rehearing and/or Petitions for Review in Docket No. ER24-232-000, et al.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 1)"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 1)"&gt;The main items agreed to in the Propel Offer of Settlement were a base return on equity of 10.3% and a total 100 basis points in ROE incentive rate adders (75 basis points for a &amp;quot;Risks and Challenges Adder&amp;quot; and 25 basis points for a &amp;quot;Grid Enhancement Adder&amp;quot;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 1)"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 1)"&gt;The Propel Offer of Settlement was certified to the Commission on September 30, 2024, and was approved by FERC on December 19, 2024. On November 20, 2023, the NYSPSC approved the Company's financing petition related to the Propel Project, Case 23-E-0390, which allows the Company to incur up to $1.4 billion in indebtedness to fund the development and construction of the Propel Project.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; page-break-after: avoid; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span style="background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 1)"&gt;On January 10, 2025, the Company submitted a request pursuant to Section 205 of the FPA in FERC Docket No. ER25-885-000 to revise its formula rate template (the &amp;quot;Formula Rate Template&amp;quot;), to i) include certain additional operation and maintenance expense accounts from the FERC Uniform System of Accounts (&amp;quot;USOA&amp;quot;) to allow for recovery of prudently incurred, transmission-related expenses and ii) establish a company-wide base ROE of 10.9% that would apply to all NY Transco transmission investment, existing and future investment, other than its investment in the Propel Project (which is set by settlement in FERC Docket No. ER24-232-000, et al.).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 1)"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 1)"&gt;On March 11, 2025, the Commission issued an order accepting the Company's request to include Accounts 561.1 through 561.8 of the USOA in its formula rate, without condition, and also accepted the 10.9% base ROE for filing, subject to refund and the outcome of hearing and settlement judge procedures, effective March 12, 2025. The Company reached a settlement with the Parties and filed an uncontested Offer of Settlement with FERC on October 2, 2025 (the &amp;quot;Transco Offer of Settlement&amp;quot;). The Transco Offer of Settlement resolves all issues set for hearing in FERC Docket No. ER25-885-000. The main items agreed to in the Transco Offer of Settlement include: (i) a base return on equity of 9.99% for all of the Company's transmission investment, other than its investment in the Propel Project; and, (ii) the ability for the Company to utilize a capital structure of 54% equity and 46% debt for all of its transmission investment other than its investment in the Propel Project for purposes of rate recovery under the Formula Rate. The Transco Offer of Settlement was certified to the Commission on November 4, 2025, and was approved by the Commission on December 22, 2025.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span style="background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 1)"&gt;In accordance with a provision in the Settlement Agreement approved by the Commission in FERC Docket No. ER15-572, the Company submitted a request on December 29, 2025, pursuant to section 205 of the FPA in FERC Docket No. ER26-893-000 to revise the depreciation rates in Attachment 9 of its Formula Rate. In that filing, the Company requested an effective date of June 1, 2026.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 1)"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 1)"&gt;On March 23, 2026, the Commission issued an order accepting the new depreciation rates, subject to refund, and set the rates for hearing and settlement judge procedures. A settlement judge has been assigned, and settlement discussions are expected to begin in April 2026.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-indent: -28.8pt; text-align: justify; page-break-after: avoid; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: &amp;quot;Arial Bold&amp;quot;; font-weight: bold"&gt;2.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 20.46pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;Summary of Significant Accounting Policies&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: justify; page-break-after: avoid; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;Basis of Presentation&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;The financial statements are prepared in accordance with the accounting requirements of the FERC as set forth in its applicable Uniform System of Accounts (&amp;quot;USOA&amp;quot;) and published accounting releases. This is a comprehensive basis for accounting other than generally accepted accounting principles in the United States of America (&amp;quot;US GAAP&amp;quot;). Differences from US GAAP include:&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 63pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-indent: -13.5pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;a.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 5.16pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;The absence of a requirement under the FERC USOA to present the current portion of long-term debt separately from the noncurrent portion of long-term debt, as required by US GAAP;&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 63pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-indent: -13.5pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;b.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 5.16pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;The presentation of federal and state income tax provision for a limited liability company electing to be treated for tax purposes as a partnership;&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 63pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-indent: -13.5pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;c.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 5.72pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;The presentation of deferred tax assets and liabilities on a gross basis in accordance with the FERC USOA, while such amounts are netted by jurisdiction and classification under US GAAP; &lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 63pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-indent: -13.5pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;d.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 5.16pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;The presentation of income taxes for operating expenses and non-operating expenses as part of operating income and other income and deductions, respectively, while such amounts are considered separately under operating income and other income and deductions for US GAAP;&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 63pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-indent: -13.5pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;e.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 5.16pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;The recognition of the equity component of a carrying charge as a regulatory asset, which is not allowed under US GAAP;&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 63pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-indent: -13.5pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;f.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 7.94pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;The treatment of land leases for transmission assets as Land and Land Rights and is included in tangible electric transmission utility plant; and&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 63pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-indent: -13.5pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;g.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 5.16pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;Operating leases in excess of 12 months are not capitalized and reported in the balance sheet accounts established for capital leases.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: justify; page-break-after: avoid; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;Use of Estimates&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;The preparation of these financial statements requires management to make estimates and assumptions that affect the reported amounts of assets and liabilities and disclosure of contingent assets and liabilities at the date of the financial statements and the reported amounts of revenues and expenses during the reporting periods. Actual results could differ from those estimates.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: justify; page-break-after: avoid; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;Regulatory Accounting&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;The FERC regulates the rates the Company charges its customers for recovery of its revenue requirement. In certain cases, the rate actions of the FERC can result in accounting that differs from nonregulated companies. In these cases, the Company defers costs (as regulatory assets) or recognizes obligations (as regulatory liabilities) if it is probable that such amounts will be recovered from or refunded to customers through future rates. Regulatory assets and liabilities are amortized to the statement of income consistent with the treatment of the related costs in the ratemaking process, which would result in a corresponding increase or decrease in future rates.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;Revenue Recognition&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;Revenue is recognized for services provided during each reporting period based on actual revenue requirements calculated using the FERC approved Formula Rate Template, and which represents the value to the customer, in settlement of the performance obligation satisfied over time. The Company accrues or defers revenues to the extent that the actual revenue requirement for the reporting period is higher or lower, respectively, than the amounts collected relating to that reporting period. The amount of accrued or deferred revenues is reflected in future revenue requirements and flows through to customer bills within two years under the provisions of the Formula Rate Template and the Formula Rate Implementation Protocols. All services provided are made available exclusively to the NYISO in accordance with the FERC guidelines.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;The Company is permitted to recover actual costs plus a return on investment under the Company's FERC approved Formula Rate Template, which establishes the charge that is to be applied for one-year periods. The Company's first Formula Rate Template was effective for 2016. By completing its Formula Rate Template on an annual basis, the Company is able to make yearly adjustments to reflect changing operating expenses and additions to utility plant when placed in service, among other items. The Formula Rate Template is the filed rate for regulatory purposes and does not require further action or any additional substantive FERC filings each year, other than a yearly annual projection with the populated template with next year estimates and true-up amounts for any over- or under-collection of expenses during the applicable rate year; and a yearly informational filing with FERC with the previous year actual revenue requirement based on the most recent completed FERC Form No. 1 data and calculation of a true-up amount for any over- or under-collection of expenses (the &amp;quot;Annual Update&amp;quot; filing, filed annually by June 30th). The rate filing for the Propel Project in FERC Docket No. ER24-232-000 did not substantively revise the Formula Rate Template or Formula Rate Implementation Protocols as the Formula Rate Template was designed to accommodate all current and future project development of the Company. The minor revisions to the Formula Rate Template proposed in FERC Docket No. ER24-232-000 were to: (i) include the Propel Project as an eligible transmission asset for cost recovery purposes; (ii) include additional Notes in an accompanying Attachment to reflect the cost containment mechanism for the Propel Project; (iii) and correct minor spelling errors. The Company also proposed a base ROE in FERC Docket No. ER24-232-000 that would apply solely to the Propel Project. The filing in Docket No. ER25-885-000 revised the Formula Rate Template slightly to include Accounts 561.1 through 561.8 of the USOA and also to establish a new base ROE value of 10.9% for all of its electric transmission investment other than its investment in the Propel Project, effective March 12, 2025.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;The recent Transco Offer of Settlement certified and approved by the Commission in Docket No. ER25-885-000 resulted in a settled rate of: 1) a base return on equity of 9.99% for all of the Company's transmission investment, other than its investment in the Propel Project; and, 2) the ability for the Company to utilize a capital structure of 54% equity and 46% debt for all of its transmission investment other than its investment in the Propel Project for purposes of rate recovery under the Formula Rate. In accordance with the Formula Rate Implementation Protocols, the template inputs included in the informational filing are subject to a yearly stakeholder review process and potential subsequent challenge at the FERC. The Company will continue to use the Formula Rate Template to calculate its annual revenue requirements unless the FERC determines any aspect of the template to be unjust and unreasonable or another mechanism is determined by the FERC to be just and reasonable.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;The Company's Formula Rate Template includes a true-up mechanism, whereby the Company compares its actual revenue requirement to the revenues collected for each year that was based on estimated costs to determine any over- or under-collection of revenue requirements (the &amp;quot;Annual True-up&amp;quot;). The Annual True-up is filed annually by June 30th, with the FERC as an informational filing.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;The NYISO collects the revenue requirement for the Company from the transmission districts within its operational control based on the FERC approved cost allocations on a daily basis. The NYISO remits the revenue requirement to the Company in accordance with its normal billing cycles.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: justify; page-break-after: avoid; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;Cash and Cash Equivalents&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;Cash and cash equivalents consist of cash held in financial institutions and is carried at cost, which approximates fair value.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;During, 2025 and 2024, the company paid $24,441,470 and $12,709,814 for interest, respectively. As of December 31, 2025 and 2024, $75,294,766&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color: rgba(255, 0, 0, 1)"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;and $76,087,513 respectively, of additions to utility plant are included in Current and Accrued Liabilities.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: justify; page-break-after: avoid; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;Allowance for Doubtful Accounts&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;The Company recognizes losses for uncollectible accounts based on specific identification of any such items. As of December 31, 2025 and 2024, the Company did not have an accounts receivable reserve.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: justify; page-break-after: avoid; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;Fair Value Measurements&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;Fair value is the price that would be received to sell an asset or paid to transfer a liability in an orderly transaction between market participants at the measurement date. The Company uses valuation techniques that maximize the use of observable inputs and minimize the use of unobservable inputs.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;Financial instruments on the balance sheets such as accounts receivable and accounts payable are stated at cost, which approximates fair value.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: justify; page-break-after: avoid; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;Utility Plant&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;Utility plant is stated at original cost. The cost of repairs and maintenance is charged to expense and the cost of renewals and betterments that extend the useful life of utility plant is capitalized as cost of additions to utility plant, which includes costs such as direct material, labor and benefits.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;During construction of the Segment B Project and the Propel Project, costs are included in construction work in progress, with 100% included in rate base for regulatory purposes in lieu of allowance for funds used during construction, consistent with the FERC approvals in Docket Nos. ER15-572-000 and ER24-232-000.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;Depreciation is computed over the estimated useful life of the asset using the composite straight-line method. Intangible electric transmission assets represent the costs of transmission assets built and paid for by the Company, which are owned and integral to and part of other electric utilities' facilities, are part of the New York bulk electric system and included in the Company's utility plant on the Formula Rate Template.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;The intangible electric transmission assets are amortized over their useful lives, which are comparable to the weighted average of tangible transmission assets, or approximately 46 years.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;During 2016, the Company entered into two 99-year leases for two of its projects totaling approximately $9.8 million. The two leases provide the Company access to certain parcels of real property and the right to attach to existing transmission structures for its transmission projects. The cost of these leases were assigned to the tangible transmission assets to which they relate and are being depreciated on a straight-line basis over 99 or 49 years.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;In March 2021, the Company entered into a 99-year lease for the NYES Project with one of its New York State utility affiliates. The cost of the lease was $39.9 million and provides the Company access to certain parcels of real property for the NYES Project and became effective on March 31, 2021. Subsequently, the Company recorded $36.6 million of these lease costs to intangible assets and are being amortized on a straight-line basis over 99 years. The remaining $3.3 million was recorded to construction work in progress.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;During 2025 and 2024, the Company recorded $0.3 million and $0.1 million for easements for the RTS and NYES Projects, which are included in intangible assets, respectively.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: justify; page-break-after: avoid; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;Debt Issuance Costs&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;Expenses incurred in connection with debt issuance are deferred and amortized over the life of the related issue.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: justify; page-break-after: avoid; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;Income Taxes&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;The Company is a limited liability company electing to be treated for tax purposes as a partnership. As such, the Members are taxed individually on their share of the Company's earnings.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;The Company is allowed to recover in its Formula Rate Template, as a component of its cost of service, the amount of income taxes that are the responsibility of the Members. The federal, state and local income tax rates are an annual input on the Formula Rate Template and are subject to the true-up mechanism. The Company is also required to adjust its rate base by the amount of deferred tax assets and liabilities it would have recorded if it were a taxable corporation.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;Income taxes are deferred, for all differences between the financial statement and the tax basis of assets and liabilities, under the asset and liability method in accordance with current accounting guidance for income taxes.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;Upon enactment of the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017 on December 22, 2017 (the &amp;quot;TCJA&amp;quot;), the Company re-measured its deferred tax assets and liabilities based upon the 21% corporate income tax rate under the TCJA that became effective on January 1, 2018. As a result, the Company decreased its net deferred tax liabilities by $5.1 million, with an offsetting regulatory liability of $5.1 million.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;Effective for tax years beginning after 2017, the TCJA reduced the federal corporate income tax rate from 35% to 21%. The Company's FERC-approved Formula Rate Template and Formula Rate Implementation Protocols required the Company to populate the 2018 Formula Rate Template in the third quarter of 2017, before passage of the TCJA, to determine the appropriate revenue requirement for calendar year 2018 and utilized a 35% federal corporate income tax rate. Accordingly, the Company deferred $3.1 million related to the over-collection of federal income taxes as a result of the change in the federal corporate income tax rate, which is normally refundable under its formula rate protocols to customers beginning in January 2020. In order to provide a more timely refund of the tax rate benefit for 2018, the Company submitted a request on June 22, 2018 with FERC for limited, one-time waivers of applicable provisions of its Formula Rate Template to enable the Company to account for the reduction in the federal corporate income tax rate under the TCJA (the &amp;quot;FERC Waiver&amp;quot;). Specifically, the FERC Waiver requested approval to provide an earlier refund to customers for the over-collection of NY Transco's tax liability that occurred in 2018. Also, the FERC Waiver sought approval to allow the Company to maintain rate base neutrality following its placement of excess accumulated deferred income taxes (&amp;quot;ADIT&amp;quot;) in accounts not included in the Company's Formula Rate Template until FERC provided further guidance as part of its Notice of Inquiry issued on March 15, 2018, Inquiry Regarding the Effect of the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act on Commission-Jurisdictional Rates, Docket No. RM18-12-000 (the &amp;quot;TCJA NOI&amp;quot;). On July 23, 2018, FERC granted the Company's waiver request. As a result of the waiver, the Company began to return the 2018 over-collection of federal tax liability in 2019 rather than 2020.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;In the TCJA NOI, FERC requested comments on the effect of the TCJA on Commission-jurisdictional rates, particularly, whether, and if so how, the Commission should address changes relating to accumulated deferred income taxes and bonus depreciation. On May 21, 2018, the Company submitted comments in response to the questions posed by FERC. Following consideration of the comments submitted in response to the TCJA NOI, FERC issued a Notice of Proposed Rulemaking (&amp;quot;ADIT NOPR&amp;quot;) on November 15, 2018, in Docket No. RM19-5-000 that proposed certain reforms to the formula or stated transmission rates of public utility transmission providers to account for the changes caused by the TCJA. On November 21, 2019, FERC issued a final rule in Docket No. RM19-5-000, Order No. 864. Order No. 864, required the Company to submit a compliance filing no later than its annual information filing on June 30, 2020. The Company filed the required compliance filing in FERC Docket No. ER20-2259-000, which included the required changes to ensure its Formula Rate Template preserves rate base neutrality and returns ADIT to ratepayers. On December 29, 2022, the Commission issued an order in Docket No. ER20-2259-000 (&amp;quot;December 29 Order&amp;quot;) in which it determined that NY Transco's proposed revisions partially complied with the requirements of Order No. 864, accepted NY Transco's proposed revisions effective January 27, 2020, and directed NY Transco to submit a further compliance filing within sixty (60) days of the order. On February 27, 2024, NY Transco submitted a compliance filing that addressed each of the Commission's directives in the December 29 Order. On May 9, 2024, the Commission issued a letter requesting, within thirty (30) days, additional information from the Company on how the proposed new Attachment to the Formula Rate would apply to any future local, state or federal tax rate change. The Company responded to the deficiency letter on June 8, 2024 with the requisite information. On July 18, 2024, the FERC issued a letter order accepting the Company's Order No. 864 compliance filings.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-indent: -28.8pt; text-align: justify; page-break-after: avoid; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: &amp;quot;Arial Bold&amp;quot;; font-weight: bold"&gt;3.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 20.46pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;Regulatory Assets and Liabilities&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; page-break-after: avoid; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;The Company recognizes regulatory assets for under-collections on its revenue requirement and regulatory liabilities for over-collections on its revenue requirement each year. As of December 31, 2025, the Company recorded $11.8 million as a current regulatory asset to be collected from customers and $4.5 million as a non-current regulatory liability for amounts to be refunded to customers. As of December 31, 2024, the Company recorded $11.3 million as a non-current regulatory asset to be collected from customers and $3.9 million as a current regulatory liability for amounts to be refunded to customers. &lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; page-break-after: avoid; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;The Company is receiving or being credited with a return on all of its regulatory assets for which a cash outflow has been made, and is paying or being charged with a return on all of its regulatory liabilities for which a cash inflow has been received.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-indent: -28.8pt; text-align: justify; page-break-after: avoid; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: &amp;quot;Arial Bold&amp;quot;; font-weight: bold"&gt;4.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 20.46pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;Utility Plant&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 28.8pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;The following table summarizes utility plant at cost along with accumulated depreciation and amortization as of December 31, 2025 and 2024:&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;table cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" style="margin-left: 35.3pt; border-collapse: collapse"&gt; 				&lt;tr style="height: 23.05pt"&gt; 					&lt;td style="width: 223.9pt; padding-right: 5.4pt; padding-left: 5.4pt; vertical-align: bottom"&gt; 						&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: justify"&gt; 							&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt; 						&lt;/p&gt; 					&lt;/td&gt; 					&lt;td style="width: 1.45pt; padding-right: 5.4pt; padding-left: 5.4pt; vertical-align: top"&gt; 						&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: center"&gt; 							&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt; 						&lt;/p&gt; 					&lt;/td&gt; 					&lt;td style="width: 61.2pt; padding-right: 5.4pt; padding-left: 5.4pt; vertical-align: middle"&gt; 						&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: center"&gt; 							&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;2025&lt;/span&gt; 						&lt;/p&gt; 					&lt;/td&gt; 					&lt;td style="width: 1.35pt; padding-right: 5.4pt; padding-left: 5.4pt; vertical-align: middle"&gt; 						&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0"&gt; 							&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt; 						&lt;/p&gt; 					&lt;/td&gt; 					&lt;td style="width: 1.45pt; padding-right: 5.4pt; padding-left: 5.4pt; vertical-align: top"&gt; 						&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: center"&gt; 							&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt; 						&lt;/p&gt; 					&lt;/td&gt; 					&lt;td style="width: 61.2pt; padding-right: 5.4pt; padding-left: 5.4pt; vertical-align: middle"&gt; 						&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: center"&gt; 							&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;2024&lt;/span&gt; 						&lt;/p&gt; 					&lt;/td&gt; 				&lt;/tr&gt; 				&lt;tr style="height: 1.2pt"&gt; 					&lt;td style="width: 223.9pt; padding-right: 5.4pt; padding-left: 5.4pt; vertical-align: bottom"&gt; 						&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: justify"&gt; 							&lt;span&gt;Intangible electric transmission&lt;/span&gt; 						&lt;/p&gt; 					&lt;/td&gt; 					&lt;td style="width: 1.45pt; padding-right: 5.4pt; padding-left: 5.4pt; vertical-align: bottom"&gt; 						&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: center; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 							&lt;span&gt;$&lt;/span&gt; 						&lt;/p&gt; 					&lt;/td&gt; 					&lt;td style="width: 61.2pt; padding-right: 5.4pt; padding-left: 5.4pt; vertical-align: bottom"&gt; 						&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: right; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 							&lt;span&gt;250,805,471&lt;/span&gt; 						&lt;/p&gt; 					&lt;/td&gt; 					&lt;td style="width: 1.35pt; padding-right: 5.4pt; padding-left: 5.4pt; vertical-align: bottom"&gt; 						&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: right; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 							&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt; 						&lt;/p&gt; 					&lt;/td&gt; 					&lt;td style="width: 1.45pt; padding-right: 5.4pt; padding-left: 5.4pt; vertical-align: bottom"&gt; 						&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: center; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; line-height: 12pt"&gt; 							&lt;span&gt;$&lt;/span&gt